  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Requirements**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the user\'s initial request. I\'m focusing on each component: the \"central engine of a smart pantry management system,\" inventory movements, ongoing cooking, shopping, and kitchen assistant functionalities. I\'m carefully breaking down these elements into discrete, manageable tasks and features.\n\n\n**Deconstructing Inventory Management**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the \"Inventory Branch\" which seems central. Stock In focuses on flexible input methods, like receipts (PDF, image, video, text, voice), extracting key data (barcode, quantity, price, expiry). Receipt extraction includes robust filtering and automated identification. Stock Out handles voice commands (\"finished the tahini\"), visual scans (empty shelves), and manual/automatic deductions based on cooking/usage.\n\n\n**Mapping Physical Pantry**\n\nI\'ve transitioned to mapping the physical pantry layout. I\'ve defined slots like `JAR_COLUMN_1` for dry goods and `CANNED_COLUMN_2` for canned items. I\'m also considering slots for backups, utensils, and perishables. This structural understanding will inform inventory visualization, and the interactive UI components, like the virtual rack and list views, which will display statuses and action buttons. I\'m prioritizing user experience with clear visual feedback.\n\n\n**Defining Cooking Processes**\n\nI\'m now deep in the cooking and procurement branches. I am handling recipe ingestion and breakdown into ingredients. I am implementing automated stock deductions (STOCK_OUT). The Zero Waste Cooking Engine is starting to take shape, it suggests recipes based on what\'s available, prioritizing items nearing expiration. I\'m also planning interactive cooking steps with timers. Then, the next task is creating dynamic shopping lists and optimal search queries for missing items.\n\n\n**Developing Shopping Lists**\n\nMy focus has shifted to the shopping list functionality, a crucial component. I\'m building it to be dynamic, generated from recipes and current inventory. I\'m focusing on features such as automated product grouping by store sections (produce, dry goods, refrigerated). The list includes price estimates and tracks purchase status (purchased/marked). I\'m also ensuring a quick transition of the shopping list to STOCK_IN with a single click.\n\n\n\n**Refining Copilot Output**\n\nI\'m now honing the output format, ensuring a structured response for every system action or user command. I\'m focusing on two key deliverables: a clear, concise user summary in Hebrew with emojis, and a structured JSON payload for internal processing. This payload will contain action types, inventory mutations, shopping list additions, financial insights, and copilot insights. It\'s designed to handle various user inputs, including text, voice, images, receipts, and videos. I\'m prioritizing user experience with clear visual feedback.\n\n\n**Defining System Commands**\n\nI\'m now expanding the system\'s command vocabulary. I am defining prepared commands, such as \"Scan Super-Pharm receipt\" (STOCK_IN), \"Voice message: \'Finished olive oil and half a pack of pasta\'\" (STOCK_OUT), \"Cooking plan: \'Making shakshuka and focaccia for 4 guests\'\" (COOKING_PLAN), \"Copilot query: \'What can I cook now with what\'s in the pantry?\'\" (Zero Waste), and \"Sync complete shopping list from shortages\" (SHOPPING_SYNC). I am also refining the structure of the system\'s code.\n\nI\'m working with a compelling split view: one side displays the pantry interface, physical slots, cooking board, shopping lists, and BI. On the other side is the \"OS Engine & Live Payload\" screen, showcasing real-time output, JSON data, and the User Summary, with options to export, copy, or apply results. I am establishing accurate TypeScript definitions for all models, slots, items, and recipes in `/src/types.ts`.\n\n\n**Implementing Mock Data & Server**\n\nI\'ve been creating extensive mock data in `/src/mockData.ts` to simulate a rich pantry with items in all slots, movement history, sample recipes, purchases, and expenses. I\'m also developing the `/server.ts` Express server, integrating the Gemini API (`gemini-3.7-flash`) with a dedicated prompt trained specifically on pantry rules, slot definitions, receipt processing, recipe parsing, and JSON formatting. I\'m building key endpoints.\n\n\n**Visualizing System Interfaces**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the design of the user interface. I\'m focusing on key views like the virtual pantry with animated visualisations for slots (e.g., `JAR_COLUMN_1`, `CANNED_COLUMN_2`, `UTILITY`, `KANBAN`, `FRESH`, `GARDEN_HERBS`). I\'m also planning the `StockMovementModal.tsx` for easy stock additions (receipts, voice, barcode), the `CookingView.tsx` for recipe management, and the dynamic shopping list in `ShoppingBranchView.tsx`.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 704551320
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
