  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Inventory Requirements**\n\nOkay, I\'m diving into the specifics. My focus is entirely on understanding the inventory requirements. Specifically, I\'m concentrating on the first component: handling stock based only on packaging units, and how it should be managed from the packaging perspective. This means only handling the inventory by packaging units and their sizes.\n\n\n**Defining Packaging Units**\n\nI\'ve established the core data structures for packaging units. In the code, `quantity` will be integers and `unit` will use Hebrew descriptions like \'\327\246\327\240\327\246\327\240\327\252\' or \'\327\221\327\247\327\221\327\225\327\247\' to represent the packaging type. I\'m focusing on simplicity: incrementing/decrementing inventory is now a matter of +/- 1 packaging unit. Next, I\'m working on the item detail modal/drawer to clearly display packaging details.\n\n\n**Integrating Cooking Module**\n\nI\'m now integrating a cooking module, focusing on quick input for meal plans through text or voice. This will then decompose recipes into simple packaging units, like \"1 tofu package\" or \"2 tomatoes.\" I\'m also preparing to present an inventory status for each ingredient in the decomposition. This should make it easy to manage both the cooking process and inventory management.\n\n\n**Implementing Inventory Updates**\n\nI\'m now implementing the update mechanism after cooking.  A \"Finish Cooking\" button will deduct used packaging units from inventory in one click, updating the status and presenting a clear summary. I am also working on the shopping list split-view, with a side-by-side display for desktop and split view for mobile, to track missing ingredients, with quick add/edit/delete options, and fast inventory updates. I am thinking of the user\'s workflow at every step!\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 621757727
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
